  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Grul felt it in his damaged flank before he saw it — the current thinning, scar tissue from the aurora-lance lighting up with a deep mineral wrongness where three furrows of muscle had never knit back. East, past the shelf-ice, past Nightfall's killing shallows, the sky was beginning to remember what light was.
    
    Green on the horizon meant the crystals above were stirring. When they woke fully, Gut Inversion's practitioners would have power again — power to reach inside an orca's body and turn the pressure-magic that held lung to rib and gut to spine into an execution. He'd watched Kzul-threk die from it at Nightfall Shelf. The corkscrew pattern etched into every burst vessel. The eyes open the whole time.
    
    "Four hours," Gork said beside him, already in his compensating spiral — the corkscrew twist of his whole body around a right fin that no longer bent where water demanded. Shadow-steel's work. "Cores cycle low. Water stays cool enough."
    
    "Then they flare."
    
    "Then they scald us."
    
    The mouth of Iasnolica exhaled copper and old char. The boundary where black water met clean sea held — a greasy meniscus, corruption too heavy to mix. Two broken hunters. Four hours. One cavern that changed its shape with each thermal cycle.
    
    Gork rolled, showing his scarred flank, and slid beneath the surface. Grul followed. The left side wouldn't turn fast enough — it never would again — so he angled his entry to compensate, driving hard with his right, and the black water closed over both of them like a throat swallowing.
    
    ---
    
    Ninety minutes in and the corridors were tighter than the kelp-charts said. Corruption had thickened the walls — not ice growth but something fibrous, black, that absorbed their echolocation clicks and returned them smeared, a half-beat late, whisper-echoes layering their pings with sounds that had no source. Gork had stopped pinging entirely. He navigated by feel, scraping his damaged fin along the right wall, letting the stone tell him where the passage bent.
    
    They hit the miners at the third junction.
    
    Four penguins with fire-hardened staves. The lead miner didn't scream — just drove the stave forward, a Nightshelf reflex, silent and immediate. Gork took the first with a jaw-strike that cracked the stave and the flipper holding it. The second went under the sled. Grul surged for the third but the corridor bent left and his flank locked and his mass carried him past the target into the wall. The aurora-crystal fragments lodged in his scarification ground against bone and shattered further — dust now, grit in the wound.
    
    The fourth miner drove a stave across the old lance-grooves. He rolled into it — the only direction his body would go — and bit through the stave and bit through what was behind it.
    
    "Move," Gork said, already past him, spiraling down the corridor. The bodies drifted behind them. Current would carry the blood deeper, where anything waiting would taste them coming.
    
    ---
    
    The chamber opened below them like a wound in the ice.
    
    Fire-cores studded the walls in irregular clusters, pulsing between dull orange and dead black. The water lurched between scalding and frigid in bands Grul could feel across his skin. Something else pressed against his insides — a torsion in the current that felt like the opening note of Gut Inversion. Residue of practice. The water remembered what it had been taught.
    
    Gork was shaking. Not from cold. The wobble running through his corkscrew meant the right fin was failing faster than it should.
    
    "There." His jaw pointed toward the far wall where the cores clustered thickest, where the corruption was so dense it was nearly solid. Break the cores and the ceiling comes down. Bury the technique in ice and rubble.
    
    The Augur materialized along the eastern wall — one moment nothing but corruption in the water, the next a shape moving like water through water. Shadow-steel in the right flipper, radiating a silence that ate echolocation whole. Grul's clicks went in and nothing came back. Not even ghosts.
    
    Gork's fin was proof he knew that blade.
    
    Grul drove at the Augur from the right, banking hard, knowing the left flank wouldn't turn fast enough if the dodge went left. The dodge went left. His strike caught cloak-edge and nothing else and the shadow-steel opened a line along his dorsal as he passed — shallow, precise, a cut that said *I could have gone deeper*.
    
    "Cores," Grul called. Meaning: *I'll keep this one busy. Finish it.*
    
    Gork spiraled toward the far wall. Twenty body-lengths. The corkscrew was worse now, the wobble pronounced, the right fin dragging. Fifteen. Ten.
    
    The Augur disengaged from Grul with a single flipper-flick — not a retreat, a dismissal — and pivoted toward Gork with a speed that made the predator-prey math invert. Shadow-steel caught the light of a pulsing core and ate it dark.
    
    Grul surged after them with everything left. Black water pushed back thick as kelp, and the inversion-residue found his insides and *squeezed*. Blood rose from his own gut — not from a wound but from the technique itself, the water practicing on whatever swam through it.
    
    Five body-lengths from the cores, Gork saw it.
    
    The Thrumed Shadow-glass. Seated in corrupted ice beside the largest fire-core cluster. Radiating a silence deeper than the blade, a silence that went down into the stone and into whatever moved beneath the frozen sea. It wasn't the mission. But his body turned toward it — the corkscrew finding the angle, the damaged fin reaching — because he was Gork Slaughterend, and he had always reached for the thing that wasn't his.
    
    His flippers closed on the glass and it scarred them immediately, frost-burn and corruption biting into the webbing, and for one moment he held it and the silence held him.
    
    The Augur's finishing stroke entered through the gap the corkscrew left exposed — the belly, the turn, the half-second where the spiral showed what was unprotected. Shadow-steel went in and didn't come out.
    
    Gork completed his last rotation with the blade inside him. The glass fell from his scarred flippers. The corkscrew unwound into drift.
    
    Grul saw it from across the chamber — saw the body slow, saw the spiral stop, saw what thirty seasons of raiding looked like when it ended. The Augur withdrew the blade and the silence withdrew with it and the water rushed in to fill the void with copper and char.
    
    The fire-cores pulsed. The thermal clock was down to minutes. The corruption pressed against his insides and blood rose from his gut, slow and steady, the water teaching his body what the penguins had learned to do.
    
    He could reach the cores. One strike, full force, and the ceiling would come down on the practice ground and the Augur and Gork's body and himself. That was the mission. That was what blood fed the deep for.
    
    Grul hung in the black water with dead aurora-crystal dust ground into his ribs and a cracked bone clicking with each breath, and he looked at the Augur standing over Gork's drifting body, and he turned his damaged flank and swam.
    
    The left side wouldn't turn fast enough. It never would. He compensated with everything the right side had left and drove for the exit channel as the cores flared behind him, thermal cycle catching, the water going from cold to scalding in bands that hit his skin like lashes. The corruption boiled. The chamber screamed with steam and the cracking of ice that had held too long.
    
    He made the outer channel on the last of it — water so shallow his dorsal scraped open against corrupted ice, the left flank slamming the channel wall once, twice, leaving skin and blood in his wake. Behind him, Iasnolica sealed its throat.
    
    The sea was black with two days left in the dark. Grul surfaced alone, bleeding from the inside, fragment-dust shining against his ribs in light that came from nowhere — just grit, just a dead trophy's memory, pressed into the wound where the lance had opened him and nothing had ever closed him right again.
    
    He breathed. The sky held nothing. He dove.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771590544717_0", anchor: "Two broken hunters. Four hours. One cavern that changed its shape with each thermal cycle.", text: "No shore-breakers driving wedges. No herders pushing groups toward killing zones. No hunt-songs at bone-ache frequency. Two maimed orcas on a demolition run is not a raid — it is the residue of a raiding culture that can no longer field proper assault formations. The standard orca tactical doctrine requires coordinated roles: breach, herd, finish. This mission has none of them. The sophistication here is that they went at all, knowing what they were. The pod doesn't abandon the broken; it spends them.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590544717_1", anchor: "not a retreat, a dismissal", text: "Nightshelf intelligence operatives during the late Incursion were briefed on orca wound profiles — which injuries restricted which axes of movement. A locked left flank means a calculable turn radius. The Augur didn't need supernatural reflexes to dismiss Grul. Just a briefing sheet and the patience to count seconds. I have made the same mistake this chronicler makes: rendering competence as mystique because the alternative is less dramatic.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590544717_2", anchor: "he had always reached for the thing that wasn't his", text: "The Shadow-glass radiates pressure-depth silence — communion with the deep, the orca ritual register. A blood-ritualist doesn't *choose* to reach for that. It calls the way deep water calls. The chronicler frames a character flaw where a compulsion is more likely, because flaws make tragedy and compulsion makes horror. Tidier narrative. Worse history.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590544717_3", anchor: "He breathed. The sky held nothing. He dove.", text: "Among penguins, there is a name for the moment after unbearable loss when the body simply goes — out onto the ice, into the dark, not returning. The Walking. It seizes those who have lost much or suffered greatly, and it is not counted as death because no one is certain that is what it is. Grul lost his pod-brother, his mission, the last crystal-dust pressed into wounds that would never close. The sky held nothing. He dove. The chronicle does not say where. I have spent a long career assuming the Walking was a penguin phenomenon — the ice, the cold, the particular way darkness settles on a flightless bird. But the deep water is also dark. And the orcas also lose. I wonder if there is a word for it in the hunt-tongue: the diving that does not stop. The chronicle's final sentence is not an ending. It is a question the chronicler did not think to ask.", type: :commentary, display: :full
